// COBOL PICTURE character-string grammar (template).
//
// Derived from IBM Enterprise COBOL for z/OS 6.4 Language Reference
// (igy6lr40.pdf), "PICTURE clause", pp. 207-224:
//   - Table 12, "PICTURE clause symbol meanings"          (pp. 208-210)
//   - "P symbol" / "Currency symbol"                      (pp. 211-212)
//   - "Data categories and PICTURE rules"                 (pp. 212-218)
//   - "PICTURE clause editing"                            (pp. 219-224)
//   - "CURRENCY SIGN clause" / "DECIMAL-POINT IS COMMA"   (pp. 129-130)
//
// THIS IS A TEMPLATE. Three terminals depend on the SPECIAL-NAMES paragraph of
// the compilation unit, so they are substituted at parser-build time:
//
//   %%CS%%      the currency symbol alternation. "The currency symbol in a
//               picture character-string is represented by the default currency
//               symbol $ or by a single character specified either in the
//               CURRENCY compiler option or in the CURRENCY SIGN clause"
//               (p. 212). SPECIAL-NAMES may hold several CURRENCY SIGN clauses,
//               each declaring a different symbol, hence an alternation.
//   %%DOT%%     the decimal point character
//   %%COMMA%%   the grouping character
//               "DECIMAL-POINT IS COMMA exchanges the functions of the period
//               and the comma in PICTURE character-strings" (p. 207) -- so the
//               two literals swap, while the rule names stay semantic.
//
// The spec's exclusion list for currency symbols (p. 129) bars digits, the
// letters A B C D E G N P R S U V X Z in either case, space, and
// + - , . * / ; ( ) " = ' -- that is, exactly the characters this grammar uses
// for other picture symbols. So a legal currency symbol can never collide with
// another terminal, and substitution cannot make the grammar ambiguous.
//
// The top-level alternatives are the USAGE DISPLAY data categories. They are
// pairwise disjoint by symbol set (see the category rules on pp. 213-218), so a
// successful parse identifies the category -- there is nothing to disambiguate.
// The multi-byte categories (DBCS, national, national-edited, UTF-8) are
// deliberately absent: they need symbols G N U that no single-byte corpus uses.
//
// Category rules encoded here, verbatim from the spec:
//   alphabetic           "can contain only the symbol A"
//   alphanumeric         ">= 1 X", or combinations of A X 9 that are neither
//                        all-A (that is alphabetic) nor all-9 (that is numeric)
//   alphanumeric-edited  symbols A X 9 B 0 / ; ">= 1 A or X, and >= 1 B or 0 or /"
//   numeric              "only the symbols 9 P S V"; S leftmost; P a continuous
//                        run in the leftmost OR rightmost positions
//   numeric-edited       symbols B P V Z 9 0 / , . + - CR DB * cs
//   external floating-pt [+|-] mantissa(9 . V) E [+|-] 99
//
// Several categories are DFA-shaped (an_a9, ae_*) rather than written the
// obvious way. That is deliberate: "contains at least one X somewhere" is a
// regular predicate, and spelling it as a state machine is what makes each
// string have exactly ONE parse tree. Written the obvious way
// (`an_pos* a_pos an_pos* nine_pos an_pos*`) the grammar is ambiguous for
// inputs like "A9A9" -- not ambiguous about the category, but ambiguous about
// the derivation, which Earley reports as a conflict.
//
// What this grammar deliberately does NOT enforce, because none of it is
// context-free -- all of it is enforced by the semantic pass over the tree
// (see picture.py, PictureAnalysis):
//   - digit-position count in 1..18 (ARITH(COMPAT)) or 1..31 (ARITH(EXTEND))
//   - total character positions <= 249
//   - "a string of at least two of the allowable floating insertion symbols"
//     (p. 222): a run's length is the SUM of its symbols' repeat counts, so
//     "$$" and "$(2)" are both floating while "$" is fixed insertion. A count
//     is a token value, not a shape, so the grammar cannot tell them apart.
//   - a floating cs run excludes a Z/* run ("Z * + - cs ... mutually exclusive
//     as floating replacement symbols", p. 224), whereas a single FIXED cs
//     before a Z/* run is legal ("$ZZ,ZZ9"). Again a length question.
//   - a floating + or - run excludes any other sign or CR/DB (p. 218)
//   - all cs tokens in one picture name the same currency symbol
//   - BLANK WHEN ZERO as the alternative to requiring an edit symbol (p. 218):
//     it is a different clause of the data description entry, not part of the
//     PICTURE character-string, so it cannot appear in this grammar at all.

start: alphabetic
     | alphanumeric
     | alphanumeric_edited
     | numeric
     | numeric_edited
     | external_float

// ------------------------------------------------------------------ alphabetic
// "The PICTURE character-string can contain only the symbol A."
alphabetic: a_pos+

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- alphanumeric
// ">= 1 X", or "combinations of the symbols A, X, and 9. (A character-string
// containing all As or all 9s does not define an alphanumeric item.)"
alphanumeric: an_has_x | an_a9

// over {A,X,9}, containing at least one X
an_has_x: an_no_x* x_pos an_any*
an_no_x: a_pos | nine_pos
an_any: a_pos | nine_pos | x_pos

// over {A,9}, containing at least one A AND at least one 9
an_a9: a_pos a9_seen_a | nine_pos a9_seen_9
a9_seen_a: a_pos a9_seen_a | nine_pos a9_both
a9_seen_9: nine_pos a9_seen_9 | a_pos a9_both
a9_both: an_no_x*

// --------------------------------------------------------- alphanumeric-edited
// "can contain the following symbols: A X 9 B 0 /. The string must contain at
// least one A or X, and at least one B or 0 (zero) or /."
// DFA over the two required-symbol flags; 9 advances neither.
alphanumeric_edited: ae_ax   ae_need_ins
                   | ae_ins  ae_need_ax
                   | ae_nine alphanumeric_edited
ae_need_ins: ae_ax ae_need_ins | ae_nine ae_need_ins | ae_ins ae_done
ae_need_ax:  ae_ins ae_need_ax | ae_nine ae_need_ax | ae_ax  ae_done
ae_done: ae_any*
ae_any: ae_ax | ae_ins | ae_nine
ae_ax: a_pos | x_pos
ae_ins: b_pos | zero_pos | slash_pos
ae_nine: nine_pos

// -------------------------------------------------------------------- numeric
// "the PICTURE character-string can contain only the symbols 9, P, S, and V.
//  The symbol S can be written only as the leftmost character in the PICTURE
//  character-string. The symbol V can be written only once."
// "The symbol P can be specified only as a continuous string of Ps in the
//  leftmost or rightmost digit positions" -- hence the two alternatives below,
//  which permit a leading run or a trailing run but never both.
// A picture of nothing but scaling positions ("P", "PPP", "SPP") has no stored
// digit position at all. NIST IX110A declares one -- 01 STATUS-TEST-10 PIC P
// VALUE ZERO -- so it is a real shape, not a theoretical one (red-dragon-m0oa.2).
numeric: SYM_S? num_core
num_core: p_run num_mantissa
        | num_mantissa p_run?
        | p_run
num_mantissa: nine_run (SYM_V nine_run?)?
            | SYM_V nine_run
nine_run: nine_pos+
p_run: p_pos+

// ------------------------------------------------------------- numeric-edited
// Symbols: B P V Z 9 0 / , . + - CR DB * cs.
//
// Fixed insertion (p. 221): "only one currency symbol and one editing-sign
// control symbol can be specified"; "unless it is preceded by a + or - symbol,
// the currency symbol must be the first character in the character-string";
// "when either + or - is used as a symbol, it must be the first or last
// character"; "when CR or DB is used as a symbol, it must occupy the rightmost
// two character positions".
//
// "Either the BLANK WHEN ZERO clause must be specified for the item, or the
// string must contain at least one of the following symbols: B / Z 0 , . * + -
// CR DB cs" (p. 218). That requirement is STRUCTURAL here, not a post-check:
// without it every plain numeric picture ("9999") also derives as
// numeric-edited with all the editing parts absent, and the two categories stop
// being disjoint. Each alternative below is one way of satisfying it: a cs is
// present, a Z/* run is present, a sign is present, a floating +/- run is
// present, or a simple/special insertion symbol sits in the body.
//
// cs_run covers fixed AND floating currency insertion in one rule, because the
// difference is the run's LENGTH (1 vs >= 2) and length is not a shape -- see
// the note at the top of this file. Same for z_run and star_run, where a
// one-symbol "run" is still zero suppression ("Specify zero suppression ... with
// a string of one or more of the allowable symbols", p. 224).
//
// A floating +/- run does need its own rule with a >= 2 lower bound: a single
// leading + or - is FIXED sign insertion, and merging the two shapes would make
// "++,+++,+++.+++" derive both as a run of eleven and as a run of ten plus a
// fixed trailing plus. "Only one of the following symbols can be written in a
// given PICTURE character-string: + - CR DB" (p. 218) is what bars the second
// reading, and giving the floating run no ne_trail slot is how that is spelled.
numeric_edited: sign_pos? cs_run ne_suppress? ne_pos* ne_trail?
              | sign_pos? ne_suppress ne_pos* ne_trail?
              | sign_pos ne_pos* ne_trail?
              | ne_float_sign ne_pos*
              | ne_bare_pos* ne_edit_pos ne_pos* ne_trail?
              | ne_bare_pos+ ne_trail

ne_float_sign: plus_float | minus_float
ne_suppress: z_run | star_run
ne_trail: sign_pos | CR | DB
sign_pos: plus_pos | minus_pos

// "Any simple-insertion symbols (B 0 / ,) within or to the immediate right of
// the string of floating insertion symbols are considered part of the floating
// character-string. If the period (.) special-insertion symbol is included
// within the floating string, it is considered to be part of the
// character-string." (p. 222)
//
// A filler may appear only BEFORE a further run symbol, never trailing the run.
// The spec calls a filler to the run's immediate right "part of the floating
// character-string", but letting the run trail fillers makes "ZZZZ.99" derivable
// two ways (the run takes ".", or ne_pos takes it). Category, digit count and
// size are identical either way, so the tie is broken here in favour of one
// parse.
cs_run:      cs_pos    (float_filler* cs_pos)*
z_run:       z_pos     (float_filler* z_pos)*
star_run:    star_pos  (float_filler* star_pos)*
plus_float:  plus_pos  (float_filler* plus_pos)+
minus_float: minus_pos (float_filler* minus_pos)+
float_filler: simple_ins | DOT

ne_pos: ne_bare_pos | ne_edit_pos
ne_bare_pos: nine_pos | p_pos | SYM_V
ne_edit_pos: simple_ins | DOT
simple_ins: b_pos | zero_pos | slash_pos | comma_pos

// --------------------------------------------------- external floating-point
// "[+|-] mantissa E [+|-] exponent"; the mantissa is drawn from 9 . V with a
// decimal point required; "the exponent must consist of the symbol 99".
external_float: sign_pos ef_mantissa SYM_E sign_pos NINE NINE
ef_mantissa: nine_pos* ef_point nine_pos*
ef_point: DOT | SYM_V

// ----------------------------------------------------------------- positions
// Every position carries an optional repeat count. "An unsigned nonzero integer
// enclosed in parentheses immediately following any of these symbols specifies
// the number of consecutive occurrences of that symbol" (p. 212). The count is
// an INT terminal, read structurally -- never sliced out of the string.
a_pos:     SYM_A count?
x_pos:     SYM_X count?
nine_pos:  NINE  count?
b_pos:     SYM_B count?
zero_pos:  ZERO  count?
slash_pos: SLASH count?
comma_pos: COMMA count?
p_pos:     SYM_P count?
z_pos:     SYM_Z count?
star_pos:  STAR  count?
cs_pos:    CS    count?
plus_pos:  PLUS  count?
minus_pos: MINUS count?

count: "(" INT ")"

// ----------------------------------------------------------------- terminals
// "The lowercase letters that correspond to the uppercase letters that
// represent the following PICTURE symbols are equivalent to their uppercase
// representations: A, B, E, G, N, P, S, U, V, X, Z, CR, DB" (p. 208). Hence the
// `i` flag on exactly those, and NOT on the currency symbol, which the spec
// calls out as "unlike all other PICTURE clause symbols, currency symbols are
// case sensitive" (p. 129).
SYM_A: "A"i
SYM_B: "B"i
SYM_E: "E"i
SYM_P: "P"i
SYM_S: "S"i
SYM_V: "V"i
SYM_X: "X"i
SYM_Z: "Z"i
CR:    "CR"i
DB:    "DB"i
NINE:  "9"
ZERO:  "0"
STAR:  "*"
SLASH: "/"
PLUS:  "+"
MINUS: "-"
INT:   /[0-9]+/
DOT:   %%DOT%%
COMMA: %%COMMA%%
CS:    %%CS%%
